{
  // Cloudflare Worker configuration for the OpenNext build.
  //
  // WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AND DID NOT BEFORE: under next-on-pages there was no wrangler
  // configuration at all - the build command and output directory lived only in the
  // Cloudflare dashboard, which is why nobody could tell from this repository how the
  // site was deployed. Everything the deployment needs is now here.
  //
  // The values for main and assets are the ones OpenNext produces and should not be
  // changed unless the build output is changed to match.
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "main": ".open-next/worker.js",

  // Also the WORKER_SELF_REFERENCE service name below, and the workers.dev subdomain
  // this deploys to. If the name is already taken in the account, change it here AND in
  // the services block - the two must match.
  "name": "geo-scanner",

  // OpenNext requires 2024-09-23 or later.
  "compatibility_date": "2024-12-30",
  "compatibility_flags": [
    // Required: the Next.js Node.js runtime needs Node APIs.
    "nodejs_compat",
    // Restricts fetch() to public URLs. The scanner fetches a hostname supplied by the
    // caller, and lib/net/fetch-safe.ts already refuses private and link-local targets in
    // application code - this enforces the same rule one layer down, where application
    // code cannot be bypassed. If a legitimate fetch ever fails with a non-public-target
    // error, the bug is in fetch-safe.ts and this flag is the thing that noticed.
    "global_fetch_strictly_public"
  ],
  "assets": {
    "directory": ".open-next/assets",
    "binding": "ASSETS"
  },

  /*
   * WORKERS CACHE, WHICH IS THE FIX FOR THE 1102 OUTAGES.
   *
   * WHY THIS LINE AND NOT A CACHE RULE. The site used to return Error 1102 intermittently, worst
   * right after a deployment, and the recorded cause is that every page view booted the Next.js
   * runtime inside the Worker against the Free plan's 10 ms CPU ceiling - cold isolates exceeded it
   * and were terminated. The obvious fix was a zone Cache Rule, and a prediction to that effect was
   * written into next.config.ts. It cannot work, and Cloudflare's own documentation says why: when a
   * Worker is on a route, the request hits the Worker BEFORE the cache is consulted, and a Cache Rule
   * has to match the URL of the Worker's outgoing fetch() rather than the visitor's request. This
   * Worker generates its responses and fetches no origin, so there is no fetch() for the rule to
   * match - and separately, "no zone configuration for caching applies to Workers Caching".
   *
   * Workers Cache sits in front of the Worker instead: a cache hit returns the response WITHOUT
   * running the Worker, so it consumes no CPU at all. It is configured here rather than in the
   * dashboard, there is no zone rule to keep in sync, and it honours the Cache-Control headers the
   * app already sets. Requires Wrangler 4.69.0 or above.
   *
   * WHY THE COMPATIBILITY DATE IS NOT BUMPED ALONGSIDE IT. Cloudflare's configuration examples
   * annotate compatibility_date with "set this to today's date", which is boilerplate for all their
   * examples rather than a requirement of this feature - the cache block is the switch. Moving this
   * Worker 21 months forward in runtime behaviour to enable caching would be a second, unrelated and
   * much riskier change. If caching does not take effect after a deploy, the date is the next thing
   * to test, and `cf-cache-status` on a second request is how to tell.
   *
   * cross_version_cache IS DELIBERATELY NOT SET. The Worker version is part of the cache key by
   * default, so every deployment starts from an empty cache - which sounds like a cost and is
   * actually correct here, because this site's deployments change page content. The cost is one
   * cache miss per URL after each deploy, collapsed by request collapsing and absorbed by the upper
   * cache tier, rather than the previous behaviour of every request being a fresh render.
   *
   * WHAT WAS CHECKED BEFORE SWITCHING IT ON: a response with NO Cache-Control is still cached
   * heuristically (200 for two hours), so a GET route that forgot the header would start serving
   * stale answers. Measured against the deployed site, every GET route sets one explicitly -
   * /api/scan and /api/llms-txt are no-store, /report/ and /monitor/report/<token>/ are no-store and
   * private, and the content pages carry s-maxage. That was true before this change; it is a
   * precondition of it, not a consequence.
   */
  "cache": {
    "enabled": true
  },
  /*
   * ⛔ DO NOT DELETE THE `geo-scanner` CLOUDFLARE PAGES PROJECT.
   *
   * There are two things called geo-scanner in the dashboard: this Worker, and a dormant
   * Pages project left over from before the migration. Deleting the Pages project looks like
   * obvious tidying, and it would take the site down.
   *
   * THE PAGES PROJECT OWNS THE DNS RECORD THIS ROUTE DEPENDS ON. Confirmed against the Pages
   * API, which reports its domains as:
   *
   *   geo-scanner-4yg.pages.dev
   *   geo-scanner.ccie13192.com      <- this one
   *
   * A route creates no DNS record. It attaches the Worker to a hostname that already resolves
   * and is already proxied, and the record that makes geo-scanner.ccie13192.com resolve is the
   * one the Pages custom domain created. Delete the project and the record goes with it,
   * leaving this route with nothing to attach to.
   *
   * ESCAPING THE DEPENDENCY, IF IT EVER MATTERS. A hostname cannot be handed from one service
   * to the other without a window in which it does not resolve, so do it deliberately and at a
   * quiet time: remove the custom domain from the Pages project (which deletes the CNAME),
   * immediately add geo-scanner.ccie13192.com here as a `custom_domain` (which makes
   * Cloudflare create a managed record), confirm with `npm run check:live`, and only then
   * delete the project. Until somebody wants that outage, the dormant project is load-bearing
   * and costs nothing.
   */
  "routes": [
    /*
     * The site's own domain. A custom_domain is right here and was impossible before.
     *
     * The contrast with the route below is the point: a custom_domain asks Cloudflare to
     * CREATE the DNS record, and it refused to do that on ccie13192.com because the Pages
     * integration had already created one. This zone was added with no records at all, so
     * there is nothing to conflict with.
     */
    {
      "pattern": "llmention-geo.com",
      "custom_domain": true
    },

    /*
     * THE OLD HOSTNAME, WHICH MUST KEEP ANSWERING.
     *
     * It is not kept out of sentiment. The reason it is a redirect rather than a deletion:
     * pages under it are indexed, the readiness badge snippets embed the origin in code
     * other people have already copied into their own footers, and both API routes carry
     * it inside a user-agent string. A hostname that stops resolving sends other people's
     * badge traffic, and real crawlers, to a domain that is not there - which is exactly
     * the failure lib/site.ts warns about.
     *
     * WHY custom_domain WAS REFUSED HERE, AND STILL WOULD BE:
     *
     *   Hostname 'geo-scanner.ccie13192.com' already has externally managed DNS records
     *   (A, CNAME, etc). Delete them first or try a different hostname. [code: 100117]
     *
     * The blocker was never the Pages binding as such, it is the CNAME that the Pages custom
     * domain integration created - which is the same record the note above is about. A route
     * creates no DNS record: it attaches this Worker to a hostname that already resolves and is
     * already proxied, which this one is.
     */
    {
      "pattern": "geo-scanner.ccie13192.com/*",
      "zone_name": "ccie13192.com"
    }
  ],
  "services": [
    {
      "binding": "WORKER_SELF_REFERENCE",
      "service": "geo-scanner"
    }
  ],

  /*
   * THE FIRST RESOURCE THIS PROJECT HAS EVER NEEDED. Everything above is a deployment
   * mechanism; this is state, and adding state is a different kind of decision.
   *
   * WHY IT IS HERE WHEN THE NOTE BELOW STILL REFUSES R2 AND IMAGES: the weekly report has
   * to remember which domains to scan and which address to send the result to. Nothing in
   * a Worker can hold that between requests, and the alternatives are worse than a
   * binding - KV cannot be queried by "everyone who is confirmed", and an external
   * database would put a second vendor and a network hop inside the cron path, which is
   * the one place with a 10 ms CPU budget.
   *
   * THE ID IS A PLACEHOLDER BECAUSE THE DATABASE DOES NOT EXIST YET. This is on the
   * `feat/subscription` branch, nothing is deployed from it, and the resource has
   * deliberately not been created. `wrangler d1 create geo-scanner-subscribers` prints
   * the real id to put here. Local development is unaffected in the meantime: `wrangler
   * dev` and `next dev` build a local SQLite from migrations/ and never contact the API.
   *
   * `migrations_dir` is what makes the schema reviewable in a diff rather than typed into
   * a dashboard. Apply locally with `npx wrangler d1 migrations apply DB --local`.
   */
  "d1_databases": [
    {
      "binding": "DB",
      "database_name": "geo-scanner-subscribers",
      "database_id": "9ab2aee2-3cd3-4be7-ae7b-e074784ae466",
      "migrations_dir": "migrations"
    }
  ]

  // DELIBERATELY ABSENT, both for now and for a stated reason:
  //
  // r2_buckets - the NEXT_INC_CACHE_R2_BUCKET binding enables OpenNext's incremental
  //   cache. Nearly every page here is produced at build time (17 content pages, 41 rule
  //   pages, 40 markdown twins), so the win has to be demonstrated before it is worth a
  //   resource and a binding. Add it here and point open-next.config.ts at the R2 cache
  //   when there is a reason to.
  //
  // images - the IMAGES binding turns on Cloudflare's image optimization. This app sets
  //   images.unoptimized and never imports next/image, so the binding would enable
  //   something nothing calls.
}
